## Define and load property
      - we write it in property file and use @PropertySource to load it in env default property source is application.property
      - and use @Value to use it
      - location of app.prop
            - classpath root src/resource etc
      - also have profile specific prop file like prod,dev,test -> app-dev.prop
      - also use yml file app.yml here space have 2 indentation from parent
      - multiple profile related data also store in single file by spring.profile property also use --- which indicates separate logic file

      - Precedence
            highest is devtool and test property because it need to override default and actual
            lowest is default in application.property
            1) devtool setting 2) @TestPropertySource and @SpringBootTest 3) command line argument 4) spring apk json 5) servlet config / context
            6) JNDI attribute from java:comp|env 7) java systeam prop 8) os env var 9)profile specific app.prop 10)app prop 11)@PropertySource file 12) apk default prop

      - map property to your class by using @ConfigurationProperties
            - @Value("${prop key}") String user;
            - we need to repeat prefix here like abc.xyz.user , abc.xyz.pass etc to overcome this 
                  create class which have annotation @ConfigurationProperties(prefix="abc.xyz") which have field like String user , String pass
                  - for make this happen we need to create a bean of it by
                        - @EnableConfigurationProperties on apk class
                        - @ConfigurationPropertyScan on apk or @Component on property class it self
            - Relexed binding
                  -  @ConfigurationProperties(prefix="sagar-desai") matches sagarDesai , sagar-desai ,sagar_desai,SAGAR_DESAi

## AutoConfiguration
      - enable it by @SpringBootApplication or @EnableAutoConfiguration
      - how does it works ?
            - it provide auto config class they are @Configuration classes with conditions
            - you can create own custom config class
            - condition includes like 
                  - do classpath contents include specific classes ?
                  - Are some properties set ?
                  - are some beans already configured ?
      - @Conditional are used to pecify condition 
      -eg   @Bean
            @ConditionalOnBean(DataSource.class)    // it only allow to create jdbc template bean if DataSource bean exist in apk context
            public JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate(DataSource ds){}
      - @ConditionalOnMissingBean/Class , @ConditionalOnClass/Property
      - @Conditional added in spring 4.0 and @Profile is an example of conditional config
      - what are auto config classes? lets take example of DataSource
            - auto-config is config class with conditions
            - pre-written spring configs are like below
                  - @AutoConfiguration
                  - public class DataSourceAutoConfiguration(){
                        @ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class) // it will only create bean if there is no bean of DataSource
                        @Import({EmbededDataSourceConfiguration.class})
                        protected stastic class EmbededDatabaseConfiguration{}
                   }
                  // this spring config execute after your custom config executed so if you written bean creation logic of datasource then spring not create it 
                     other wise spring create it's bean by default
                  - this config classes you may found in spring-boot-autoconfigure/META-INF/spring jar file 
                  - inside org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports
                  - beans you define always take precedence over AutoConfiguration



